.TH ENCRYPTOR 1 "November 2026" "Encryptor 3.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
encryptor \- authenticated file encryption and X.509 toolkit built on OpenSSL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B encryptor
.br
.B encryptor encrypt
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B encryptor decrypt
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B encryptor inspect
[\fB\-\-json\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B encryptor verify
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B encryptor certs
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-warn\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B encryptor audit
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B encryptor selftest
|
.B passgen
[\fILENGTH\fR] |
.B completion
\fIbash\fR|\fIzsh\fR |
.B help
|
.B version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B encryptor
encrypts files with a password or for the holders of X.509 certificates.
Without arguments it starts an interactive, keyboard-driven interface; with a
command it is a non-interactive tool suited to scripts and CI pipelines.
.PP
New files are written in the self-describing
.B ENCRYPTOR/3
container: a stream cipher or block cipher combined with HMAC-SHA256
(encrypt-then-MAC). The authentication tag covers the header and all
ciphertext. The file is read once, and output is released only if the tag over
the bytes that were decrypted is correct: a wrong password, a modified file,
or a file altered while it is being read never produce output.
.PP
Password mode generates a random file key and seals it under the password
with scrypt (N=16384, r=8, p=5; memory-hard), or derives the key with
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (600,000 iterations) when \fB\-\-kdf pbkdf2\fR is given.
Recipient mode generates a random file key and wraps it for each certificate
with CMS (RSA-OAEP or ECDH). The parameters are stored in the file.
.PP
Passwords and keys are passed to
.BR openssl (1)
through anonymous pipes: they never appear in a process argument list and are
never written to disk.
.PP
Files written by Encryptor 2.x (raw
.B openssl enc
output, or S/MIME) carry no authentication. They are refused unless
\fB\-\-legacy\fR is given, and should be re-encrypted.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B encrypt
Encrypt each \fIFILE\fR to \fIFILE\fR.enc. Uses recipient mode when
\fB\-\-recipient\fR is given, otherwise password mode.
.TP
.B decrypt
Decrypt each \fIFILE\fR. The format, cipher and key type are read from the
file. The default output name removes the .enc suffix.
.TP
.B inspect
Print the container metadata. No password or key is needed.
.TP
.B verify
Check the password or key and the authentication tag; writes nothing.
.TP
.B certs
Report on the certificates in the store. Exit status 1 if any certificate is
expired or expires within \fB\-\-warn\-days\fR (default 30).
.TP
.B audit
Run the local security audit (self-test, permissions, key protection,
certificate expiry and strength, legacy files). Exit status 1 if a check fails.
.TP
.B selftest
Run known-answer tests for HMAC-SHA256, PBKDF2 and the scrypt key envelope,
round trips and a tamper test against the installed OpenSSL.
.TP
.B passgen
Print a random password of \fILENGTH\fR characters (default 24).
.TP
.B completion
Print a shell completion script.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIPATH\fR
Output file; "\-" writes to standard output. Only with a single input.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pass\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the password from the first line of \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-pass\-env\fR \fIVAR\fR
Read the password from environment variable \fIVAR\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-pass\-stdin\fR
Read the password from the first line of standard input.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recipient\fR \fICERT\fR
Encrypt for the PEM certificate \fICERT\fR. May be repeated.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
Private key used to decrypt a recipient-mode file.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cert\fR \fICERT\fR
Certificate matching \fB\-\-key\fR (optional).
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-pass\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Passphrase of an encrypted private key.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-algo\fR \fICIPHER\fR
One of aes\-256\-ctr (default), chacha20, aes\-256\-cbc, camellia\-256\-cbc,
aria\-256\-cbc. When decrypting a legacy 2.x file: the cipher it was written with.
.TP
\fB\-\-kdf\fR \fINAME\fR
Password key-derivation function for new files: \fBscrypt\fR (memory-hard;
the default when the OpenSSL build supports it), \fBpbkdf2\fR (NIST-approved)
or \fBauto\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-iter\fR \fIN\fR
PBKDF2 iterations (minimum 100000, default 600000). Implies \fB\-\-kdf pbkdf2\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\fR
Accept an Encryptor 2.x file. Such files cannot be verified: use this only for
files whose origin you trust. Without it they are refused with exit status 6.
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\-iter\fR \fIN\fR
Iterations of a legacy file: 600000 for 2.1 (default), 100000 for 2.0.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite an existing output file.
.TP
\fB\-\-shred\fR
After the encrypted copy has been verified, overwrite and delete the input.
With \fB\-\-recipient\fR, \fB\-\-key\fR must name a recipient's private key so
that the copy can be opened. Best effort only on SSDs and copy-on-write or
journaling filesystems.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print one JSON object per file on standard output.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational output.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colors. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable is also honoured.
.PP
A \fIFILE\fR of "\-" reads standard input. When no password option is given
and a terminal is attached, the password is prompted without echo.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Wrong password or key, or the data failed authentication.
.TP
.B 4
Input not found or unreadable.
.TP
.B 5
Output exists (without \fB\-\-force\fR) or cannot be written.
.TP
.B 6
Unsupported or unrecognised format.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENCRYPTOR_CONFIG_DIR
State directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/encryptor or ~/.config/encryptor).
.TP
.B ENCRYPTOR_KDF
Password KDF for new files: scrypt, pbkdf2 or auto.
.TP
.B ENCRYPTOR_PBKDF2_ITER
Default PBKDF2 iteration count.
.TP
.B ENCRYPTOR_NO_LOG
Set to 1 to disable the operation log.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/encryptor/certs/
Certificates (.pem), private keys (.key, mode 400), requests (.csr), bundles (.p12).
.TP
.I ~/.config/encryptor/encryptor.log
Operation log (mode 600, rotated at 1 MiB). Records operations and file
names; never passwords, keys or content.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt with a prompted password:
.PP
.RS
encryptor encrypt report.pdf
.RE
.PP
Scripted backup of a directory:
.PP
.RS
tar cz project/ | encryptor encrypt \-p pass.txt \-o project.tgz.enc \-
.br
encryptor decrypt \-p pass.txt \-o \- project.tgz.enc | tar xz
.RE
.PP
Encrypt for two people, then decrypt as one of them:
.PP
.RS
encryptor encrypt \-r alice.pem \-r bob.pem contract.docx
.br
encryptor decrypt \-k ~/.config/encryptor/certs/alice.key contract.docx.enc
.RE
.PP
Monitor certificate expiry from cron:
.PP
.RS
encryptor certs \-\-json \-\-warn\-days 45 || notify\-admin
.RE
.SH SECURITY NOTES
There is no password recovery. Choose a long passphrase
(\fBencryptor passgen\fR); scrypt slows guessing but cannot rescue a weak one. Recipient mode provides confidentiality
and integrity but does not identify the sender. Secure deletion cannot be
guaranteed on modern storage; use full-disk encryption.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR openssl\-enc (1),
.BR openssl\-cms (1),
.BR age (1),
.BR gpg (1)
